{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Norman ruler who was the last ruler of County of Tripoli before its fall in 1271. He was born in 1148 in Normandy and died on April 11 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Ponsa of Toulouse. \n\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III succeeded his father as the ruler of County of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's death. He married Agnes of Sidon in 1163 and had several children with her. He adopted the rule of County of Tripoli and expanded the county's territories by conquering the County of Jerusalem from 1152 to 1154 and the County of Chastel-Noble in 1157. He also established a powerful navy to protect the county's shipping interests.\n\n**The First Crusade and the Battle of Hattin**\nIn 1177 Raymond III joined the Second Crusade but was defeated at the Battle of Hattin in 1187. The Crusaders were defeated by the Ayyubid forces led by Saladin who then captured Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. The defeat led to Raymond III's flight to the Kingdom of Sicily.\n\n**The Fall of County of Tripoli**\nAfter the fall of County of Tripoli in 1271 Raymond III died in exile in the Kingdom of Sicily. The county was then ruled by the Kingdom of Sicily as a fief. The County of Tripoli ceased to exist as an independent entity but its legacy continued through the County of Jerusalem and the Kingdom of Tripoli which were ruled by the Rais of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli played an important role in the history of the Crusades and the County of Tripoli. He was a skilled military leader and an effective ruler who expanded the county's territories and established a powerful navy. Despite his defeat at the Battle of Hattin he continued to fight until the end of his life in exile.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.57627811808924